WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:18.010
Four thousand five hundred years ago, the Egyptians built the pyramids of Giza as grand and impressive tombs for their pharaohs.

2
00:00:18.011 --> 00:00:28.698
The largest of these enormous monuments is almost 147 meters high, and for four thousand years, it was the tallest man-made structure on earth.

3
00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:32.977
Today the tallest man-made structures are skyscrapers.

4
00:00:32.978 --> 00:00:40.118
Every day in cities around the world, millions of people go to work in towers made of steel and glass.

5
00:00:40.119 --> 00:00:46.420
These modern structures exhibit the best in construction technology and engineering skills.

6
00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:50.116
And the modern city could not function without them.

7
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:58.492
When the demand for living and working space in the limited area of a city's downtown grows, there is nowhere to build but up.

8
00:00:58.493 --> 00:01:03.520
Smaller buildings are demolished, and taller buildings are built to replace them.

9
00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:12.294
The streets of Manhattan in New York City, where real estate for new buildings is limited, are lined with tall, gleaming office towers.

10
00:01:12.295 --> 00:01:18.501
During the first half of the nineteenth century, buildings were rarely taller than five stories.

11
00:01:18.502 --> 00:01:25.623
Buildings higher than this would have been impractical—office workers would have become exhausted from climbing so many stairs!

12
00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:31.579
Then in 1853, Elisha Otis invented the safety elevator.

13
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.799
Previous elevators were used mostly for freight, not people.

14
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.668
If the cable that held the elevator snapped, the elevator would fall, killing those inside.

15
00:01:41.669 --> 00:01:49.311
The safety elevator has mechanical locks that automatically hold the elevator firmly in place if the cable snaps.

16
00:01:49.312 --> 00:01:54.506
The invention of the safety elevator made tall buildings a practical reality.

17
00:01:54.507 --> 00:02:01.092
While Elisha Otis was improving the elevator, other inventors were hard at work improving water pumps.

18
00:02:01.093 --> 00:02:07.390
As cities expanded, more powerful pumps were needed to provide water to their distant areas.

19
00:02:07.391 --> 00:02:15.987
Eventually pumps became powerful enough to supply water to great heights, enabling cities to grow upward as well as outward.

20
00:02:15.988 --> 00:02:24.804
In the middle of the nineteenth century, manufacturers developed the process for mass-producing steel, which is stronger and lighter than iron.

21
00:02:24.805 --> 00:02:31.697
With steel beams, buildings could be built around a metal frame, rather than out of stone walls.

22
00:02:31.698 --> 00:02:35.386
Stone walls are impractical for very tall buildings.

23
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:42.968
If stone blocks are piled too high one on top of another, their combined weight will crush the bottom blocks.

24
00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:53.243
Steel-frame construction and improvements like the safety elevator and powerful water pumps allowed buildings to soar higher than ever before.

25
00:02:53.244 --> 00:02:58.360
Soon builders were competing to construct taller and taller skyscrapers.

26
00:02:58.361 --> 00:03:04.709
When Walter Chrysler, founder of an automobile company, started work on the Chrysler Building in New York,

27
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.364
he wanted it to be the tallest building in the world.

28
00:03:08.365 --> 00:03:15.086
But during its construction, work began on an even taller building—the Bank of Manhattan Building.

29
00:03:15.087 --> 00:03:22.526
Chrysler, however, had kept something secret: inside his building a giant spire was under construction.

30
00:03:22.527 --> 00:03:30.010
After the Bank of Manhattan Building was finished in 1930, the spire was placed on top of the Chrysler Building.

31
00:03:30.011 --> 00:03:37.956
It extended the building's height to about 319 meters, making it the world's tallest building.

32
00:03:37.957 --> 00:03:42.480
But the competition for the tallest skyscraper did not end there.

33
00:03:42.481 --> 00:03:49.289
A year later the Empire State Building was finished, standing almost 450 meters high.

34
00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:57.207
When it became possible to build skyscrapers hundreds of meters tall, unusual engineering challenges surfaced.

35
00:03:57.208 --> 00:04:02.142
Strong winds can cause a tall building to sway back and forth.

36
00:04:02.143 --> 00:04:09.601
To stop the building from shaking, many skyscrapers have a central core of steel around the main elevator shaft.

37
00:04:09.602 --> 00:04:12.620
This makes the skyscraper more rigid.

38
00:04:12.621 --> 00:04:16.844
Also skyscrapers must use a special type of glass.

39
00:04:16.845 --> 00:04:21.359
Tempered glass, or safety glass, is stronger than ordinary glass.

40
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.773
When safety glass breaks, it shatters into small pieces with rounded edges.

41
00:04:26.774 --> 00:04:37.068
Ordinary glass in a skyscraper window would be hazardous; if it broke, large, sharp pieces would fall and injure people on the sidewalks below.

42
00:04:37.069 --> 00:04:41.147
Tremendous skill is required to build skyscrapers.

43
00:04:41.148 --> 00:04:47.837
The steel beams must be riveted and welded together before the floors and walls can be put in place.

44
00:04:47.838 --> 00:04:53.444
Before workers were required to wear safety harnesses, it was very dangerous work.

45
00:04:53.445 --> 00:05:00.403
One mistake or an unexpected gust of wind could send a construction worker falling to his death.

46
00:05:00.404 --> 00:05:07.324
For much of the twentieth century, the Mohawk Indians were considered some of the best construction workers.

47
00:05:07.325 --> 00:05:17.478
Many of them fearlessly strode along the narrow beams of steel, hundreds of meters off the ground, earning the nickname skywalkers.

48
00:05:17.479 --> 00:05:20.930
New skyscrapers are still being built.

49
00:05:20.931 --> 00:05:27.541
As of 2014, the tallest skyscraper on earth is the Burj Khalifa in Dubai.

50
00:05:27.542 --> 00:05:32.913
It is 828 meters tall and was completed in 2009.

51
00:05:32.914 --> 00:05:41.014
In 2014 plans to construct an even taller skyscraper in Saudi Arabia—the Kingdom Tower—were announced.

52
00:05:41.015 --> 00:05:48.262
If completed, the Kingdom Tower will have three hundred floors and reach one kilometer into the sky!

53
00:05:48.263 --> 00:05:56.032
Thanks to advances in materials and technology, today's skyscrapers stand many times higher than the pyramids.

54
00:05:56.033 --> 00:06:01.031
And architects and engineers keep dreaming about even taller structures.

55
00:06:01.032 --> 00:06:15.502
Future man-made structures will likely reshape the urban landscape—just as the first skyscrapers did over a hundred years ago.
